DILG Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG-NAPOLCOM Center, EDSA cor. Quezon Avenue, West Triangle, Quezon City www.dilg.qov.ph DILG OPINION No. 03 s. 2022 Jan. 18, 2022 VICE MAYOR WILMA R. CANAMA Tangub City Province of Misamis Occidental Dear Vice Mayor Canama: This has reference to your letter dated 3 December 2021 , requesting the Department's opinion on issues regarding the two resolutions approved by the Sangguniang Panlungsod of Tangub City, Misamis Occidental. Based on your narration, the following issues are being raised: 1. Whether or not you should affix your signature signifying approval of the resolutions approved by the SP, denying the request for extension of time filed by the respondent and the resolution passed by the SP recommending for the preventive suspension of the respondent; 2. Whether or not the service of the Notice and Order made by the Sangguniang Panlungsod Secretary to the respondent Punong Barangay is valid; and 3. Whether or not the resolutions denying the request for extension of time filed by the respondent and the recommendation of the SP for the preventive suspension of the respondent Punong Barangay is valid. Per your letter you assumed as Vice-Mayor of Tangub City, being the highest ranking Sangguniang Panlungsod Member, when the elected Vice-Mayor died on November 2, 2021. upon assumption you were confronted with administrative complaints filed against elected Punong Barangays of the City of Tangub. However, without the complaints being deliberated upon by the Sangguniang Panlungsod (SP), the Secretary to the SP, without her and the SP's authority, issued a Notice and Order requiring the respondents to submit their respective answers. Under Article N of Resolution No. 2004-06-1244 of the Quasi-JudiciaI Function of the Sanggunian, it is the SP, thru its Presiding Officer (PO) who is authorized to issue an order requiring the respondents to submit his/her verified answer. Further it is your view that the act of the SP in immediately denying the request for an extension of time filed by the respondents is unjustified, as well as the act of the SP in immediately recommending for the preventive suspension of the respondents, even without hearing to determine whether the evidence of guilt is strong and whether there exist a great possibility that the respondent's continuance in office could influence the witnesses or pose a threat to the safety and integrity of the records and other evidence. Hence, your request for an opinion. "Matino, Mahusay at Maasahan" Trunkline No.: 8876-34-54

Before answering the first issue, this Office reiterates its opinion in DILG Legal Opinion No. 092-03, dated 7 August 2003, where the Department has opined that the Vice- Governor in his capacity as the Presiding Officer does not have authority to refuse to attest an ordinance or resolution which has already been duly approved or adopted by the Sangguniang Panlalawigan. The power of attestation of the Presiding Officer is constricted only to affirm the fact of the approval by the majority of the sanggunian of what has been submitted by him to the sanggunian. Clearly, the Presiding Officer does not have the authority to determine for himself the propriety of the approval of a particular legislative measure as his function is purely mechanical act of seeing to it that the resolution or ordinance is approved by the numerical majority of the sanggunian. Otherwise, the majority of the sanggunian can be held captive by the whims of the Presiding Officer which obviously runs counter to the basic tenet that "majority rules". Such that, the Presiding Officer cannot, in the pretext of claiming to be representing in behalf of the sanggunian, substitute his judgment for that of the majority of the sanggunian. In the case herein, the Vice-Mayor is the Presiding Officer of the Sangguniang Panlungsod pursuant to Section 49 of the Local Government Code. Now, as to the first issue, the Department qualifies. In your letter, it was not clear whether you are the Vice-Mayor and Presiding Officer during the approval of said resolutions. If at the time of the approval of the resolutions denying the request for extension of time filed by the respondent PBs and the recommendation of the SP for the preventive suspension of the respondent PBS, you are the Presiding Officer, then, it becomes your ministerial duty as the Vice-Mayor and Presiding Officer to attest the same. However, if you are still a member of the Sangguniang Panlungsod at the time the resolutions were approved, then you cannot attest to the fact of its approval since such duty is lodged to the Presiding Officer. Anent to the second issue, the Department concurs with your stand. The SP Secretary has no authority to issue to the respondent PBs the said Notice and Order to file their verified answer. The Local Government Code (LGC) vested the Sangguniang Panlungsod (SP), in the exercise of its quasi-judicial functions, the authority to discipline elected barangay officials. The exercise of such quasi-judicial function is found in Sections 60 to 68 of the Local Government Code. However, the LGC is silent on the manner of issuance of notice and order to the parties to the complaint. In the absence thereof, the IRPI of the Sangguniang Panlungsod of Tangub City shall govern. 1 DILG Opinion No. 68 s. 2019 dated October 14, 2019 explained that Section 50 of the Local Government Code, the Sanggunian is given the authority to promulgate its own Internal Rules of Procedure. Accordingly, the conduct of the internal affairs of that sanggunian should be done in accordance with law, primarily, and by the adopted Rules, if the law is silent. Page 2 of 3

Article N of the IRP specifically states that the SP, thru its Presiding Officer (PO), is authorized to issue an order requiring the respondents to submit his/her verified answer. Consequently, the act of the SP Secretary, without official instructions from the SP through its Presiding Officer, is considered ultra vires. The SP Secretary cannot act on its own since the exercise of quasi-judicial function is exclusively lodged with the As to the third issue on the propriety of the approval of the two resolutions, the issue presents a contentious matter which requires adjudication in the proper forum. The Department shall defer from addressing said issue. This opinion is being rendered without prejudice to any other contrary opinion of more competent authorities. We hope to have sufficiently addressed your concerns. Thank you. Very truly yours, THORITY OF THE SECRETARY: AN MARLO reta for Local Government LLLS/RPB/LRALLD/rrao/0D/gms cc: DIR. ARNEL M. AGABE, CESO Ill, Regional Director, DILG-Region X Page 3 of 3 DILG ;
